In article <20puvr$h3s@unicorn.nott.ac.uk>, cczanj@vaxb.nott.ac.uk (Andy Jack) writes:
> We have an elderly mVAX II with an RA82 attached that has recently begun to
> play up. The fault light comes on and when I press the button twice the Write
> Protect and B loghts flash. The book suggests this is a "Servo Fine Positioning
> Error" or some such. If I power the thing off and leave it for a few hours, the
> problem goes away but soon comes back again. It ran long enough to take a
> backup.
> 
> It's not on maintenance and not offering a service. I was, however, hoping to
> do some DECnet/OSI practicing with it so feel agrieved. Is it likely to be
> completely bust or can I do some reformatting or something? 

  Well, you could open the top hatch (best done when the drive is powered off,
as the little metal grommet pieces fall off and short out the logic board).
Inside you will see a DB-25 connector (next to the large start capacitor). Do
not plug a terminal in yet. Instead, power up the drive first (otherwise the
drive will usually fail to power up). Now, hook up a terminal set for 300 baud.
Proceed as usual to try to boot, whatever until the drive faults. Now, press
the A or B buttons so that both are out. Type ^C on the terminal. You should
get an RA8x> prompt. Type SHOW DIAGNOSTIC FAULTS (or is it SHOW DIAG FAULT -
it's been a while. Oh well. It knows what HELP is, at least). It will give you
a list of errors and the most likely failing module (probably servo, HDA, and
read-write). More information can be obtained by spinning the drive down,
clearing the fault, and saying RUN DIAG on the terminal. You'll get lots of
test results, and if all is OK, spin the drive up and do RUN DIAG again. Send
me mail with the results of all this and I'll let you know what the books say
about your fault.

  On the other hand, if it is an HDA fault, you will probably find the drive
isn't worth fixing.

