This makes about four queries like this in the last month (though usually
it's the other way around -- "can I use this old DEC monitor on a PC?").  
So, I'm reposting my article on DEC workstation video standards.  The GPX is
"type 1" in my table.  

--- Jamie Hanrahan, Kernel Mode Systems, San Diego CA
drivers, internals, networks, applications, and training for VMS and Windows NT
uucp 'g' protocol guru and release coordinator, VMSnet (DECUS uucp) W.G., and 
Chair, Programming and Internals Working Group, U.S. DECUS VMS Systems SIG 
Internet:  jeh@cmkrnl.com (JH645)  Uucp: uunet!cmkrnl!jeh  CIS: 74140,2055

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(HELP ME WHITEWASH THIS FENCE!  See the list of "additional information
needed" at the end.)  

timing info for DEC's VAXstation video standards, and the machines with which
they are usually associated:

typical applications		h res	v res	h rate	v rate	type #
                                                                (see notes)
GPX (VAXstation I, II, 3n00)	1024	864	54 kHz	60 Hz	(1)
SPX (VAXstation 3100/76)	1280	1024	61 kHz	66 Hz	(2)
VS 4000/60,/90,/VLC; AXP(all?)	1280	1024	77 kHz	72 Hz	(3)
VS 4000/VLC 60 Hz (some models)	1024	768	?	60 Hz	(4)
VS 4000/VLC 72 Hz (standard)	1024	768	?	72 Hz	(5)

Note that the timing info (not just the resolution) is critical in determining
monitor/workstation compatibility.  For example, there are some monitors which
are (for example) advertised as capable of 1280x1024, and of supporting 72 Hz
vertical sweep, but if you read the fine print in the manual you find that they
won't run at the *combination* of 1280 x 1024 x 72 Hz. ie you need to check not
just the ranges of the individual parameters but also the combinations that are
supported. 

Notes:

(1) As far as I know this is DEC's original workstation video standard.  In
addition to the "GPX" card set (VCB02) that went in Q-bus VAXstations, this
standard is also used by the single-plane VCB01 (QVSS), by the
"on-the-motherboard" video controller on the VAXstation 3100 series, and also
by the GPX add-on graphics option for VAXstation 3100.  Monitors include VR150,
VR260, and VR262 monochrome, and the VR160, 290, 292, 297, and 299 color.  As
far as I know, no current machines use this standard. 

(2) Introduced with the SPX option on the VAXstation 3100 Model 76.  Monitors
sold for use with this include VR319 (b&w), VR320 (color), and VRT19
(Trinitron).  In addition to the VAXstation 3100/SPX, early VAXstation
4000/60's that were purchased as "upgrades" from a 3100 SPX system came with a
special video card that uses this standard.  This was done so that the customer
could continue to use the 66 Hz-only monitor (often a VRT19) that came with the
3100 SPX. 

(3) Later 4000/60's reportedly come with a video board that does either type 2
or type 3 -- 66 Hz or 72 Hz, 1280x1024.  According to reports, the board does
the right thing depending on whether you use the D-15 connector (66 Hz) or the
newer three-coax-in-a-D-shell connector (72 Hz).  I haven't seen this myself,
and I have seen counterexamples, but who knows how many versions of the 4000/60
there are?  ...hopefully someone from DEC workstation engineering will fill us
in.  The 4000/90 seems to only be available with the 72 Hz version.  Later
versions of DEC 1280x1024 monitors (VRT19, VR319, and VRM17) can handle both 72
and 66 Hz vertical rates.  These variants are identified by suffixes on the
model number (eg VRT19-XX).  I don't have details as to which versions support
what.  

As far as I know, all Alpha/AXP-based workstations use type 3 also.  

(4), (5) 4000/VLCs seem to be available with three different video standards.
Type 4 (1024x768, 60 Hz) is intended for low-cost configurations, as it will
work with most "super VGA" monitors intended for PCs (such as the Sony 1304),
as long as the sync is compatible -- see below.   

Sync notes -- unlike PC video, DEC RGB video invariably uses sync-on-green,
instead of separate sync connections.  In addition, many of the PC video
standards use different combinations of inverted or normal horizontal and
vertical sync (four different combinations) to give the monitor a hint as to
the sweep rates being used.  DEC does not do this.  Except for the timing,
their "green" signal always looks like standard RS170 composite video, with
"blacker than black" sync tips. 

Impedance:  The DEC monitors' RGB inputs are terminated with 75 ohms, and 75
ohm cables should be used.  A few early DEC monitors had "loopthrough" 
connections with switchable termination, but current models don't.  

Compatibility (non-DEC monitors/DEC workstation):

In article <1993Feb9.143425.21049@tigger.jvnc.net>, watersb@pt.cyanamid.com 
(watersb) writes:
> Can one put a standard multisync RGB monitor on a Vaxstation 4000/60
> and make it work, or do i have to use a VRTnn type monitor from DEC??

Ummmm... that depends on what you mean by a "standard multisync RGB monitor".
First, let's call it a "multistandard monitor" instead, since "multisync" is a
trademark of NEC.  That aside, there isn't any "standard" that I know of with
respect to the sweep rates that are supported by such monitors.  There are
definitely some monitors advertised as "multisync" which will work on PCs at
standard VGA and a few "super VGA" rates, but which won't work on most DEC
machines, especially not on the not on the 72 Hz machines.  

You also need to make sure that the monitor will accept sync on green -- DEC
video cards don't emit separate sync signals.  the Sony and NEC monitors I've
checked all will do so.  

The NEC MultiSync 5FG and 6FG definitely *will* work on the VAXstation 4000/60.
And on every other DEC video standard.  And on most every PC or Mac standard up
through 1280x1024.  (These monitors give *spectacular* images; in my opinion
the 6FG, street price $2400, would be a much better buy than DEC's VRT19, even
if you could get the VRT19 for the same price (DEC's list is about $4K).  If
you have a choice, order your VAXstation or DECstation without the DEC monitor,
and buy one of these NEC monitors instead.) 

The smaller NEC monitors and the new lower cost version of the 5FG (5FGx, I
think) will NOT work, even though they're called "multisync".  They won't run
fast enough.  So just the "multisync" appellation isn't enough, even on a NEC
monitor.  

Compatibility (DEC monitors/non-DEC machines):

Lots of people ask:
> ~"I have this DEC monitor that came off of a VAXstation nnn; can I use it
> on a PC or Mac?"

The general answer is "probably not, at least not without a lot of trouble". 

First you need a video card that generates signals with geometry and timing 
as shown in the above table.  The standard SVGA for PCs is 1024x768, ie 768
scan lnies, not 864 as is used by the older DEC monitors, so you may be 
stopped right there.  

If you do find a card that can be programmed to generate the proper timing, you
aren't out of the woods yet.  Your PC or Mac video card probably does not emit
sync on green, and that's what the DEC monitor wants. 

You may also have a problem in that most PC SVGA video cards, even those that
spend most of their life at 1280x1024, come up in standard VGA mode,
1024x768x60 Hz.  The DEC monitor may not like this.  If it doesn't you won't
be able to see anything until your o/s boots up far enough to excecute the
command to change the video card mode from the default.  

Needed additional information:

	Any errors you notice in the preceding

	Details on sweep rates supported by DEC monitor "sub-models"

	Confirmation on AXP workstation video

	Information on DECstation 3000 (MIPS Ultrix) workstations

	Success stories -- what third-party monitors have you gotten to work
	 on what DEC workstations?  
	 What PC or Mac video cards have you gotten to work with which DEC 
	 monitors, and what did you have to do to acheive it?

	Anything else you think is relevant

