	KAP's pretty bogus unless you're trying to crack a benchmark.
I'm amazed that anyone accepts KAP-ized benchmark figures in the first
place. KAP-ized codes for non-benchmark applications have been known 
to run faster in some cases, incorrectly in others, or the same. DEC,
at least, wasn't the vendor that started the whole KAP scam rolling.
They had the choice of either looking slow against their competitors
who were cracking the benchmark, or crying "FOUL!"  Unfortunately,
the trade press didn't do the right thing and cry "FOUL!" when IBM
and HP started coming up with ridiculous values for MATRIX on the
SPEC suite.

	I personally consider benchmark-oriented optimizations to
be bordering on dishonest, and I no longer trust published figures
from any corporation that publishes values of KAP-ized benchmarks.

	Someone who'd sell you a system based on KAP-ized benchmarks
is the same kind of person who'd sell you a "high performance" car
because it has been benchmarked at a steady 1G acceleration - and
won't mention that it was when they pushed it off a cliff.

	With the trade press lacking the cojones to call the vendors
on their deceit, our only option as potential buyers is to force the
vendors to let us run OUR applications on their box, using a stock
configuration and the default compiler settings. Notice what happens
when you do that? Sometimes the blindlingly fast machine looks quite
mediocre. Sometimes the mediocre machine actually performs quite well.

	The SPEC cooperative lost all credibility when they didn't
cry "FOUL!" over KAP. What a damn shame.

mjr.


