Not necessarily.  As I recall, the one and only optimization you have to
perform on matrix300 to make it run faster (and I don't think I'm giving out
any HP trade secrets here) is recognize the two dimensional array is being
traversed in row major rather than column major order, and rewrite the loop
accordingly, which dramatically improves locality and hence cache performance.
That's it.  This seems to me to be an inherently useful optimization - at
least, for programmers who were not already careful about such things - and
should pretty standard fare for any vectorizing compiler, I would think.

> SPEC blew it because they didn't blow the
> whistle on this game, and now they have lost any credibility
> they might have had.

I fault the SPEC committee for including a benchmark can be so easily
circumvented by such a naturally useful optimization technique.

