In article <TWILLEY.93Jun15092428@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil>, twilley@dewey.nl.nuwc.navy.mil (Jack Twilley) writes:
> I was wondering if anyone here has a utility that will allow VMS users
> to 'rsh' to a Ultrix machine connected via DECnet and/or UCX 1.3 from
> the command line.

You should check out /usr/examples/decnet/TELL.COM and /usr/bin/tell.
Or just type in:  csh> man tell

From the VMS side, the syntax is:

	$ TELL ULTRIX-NODE "ultrix-command"

From the Ultrix side, the syntax is:

	csh> tell vms-node vms-command


To use TELL from VMS to Ultrix, make sure you've got object TELL defined
in your NCP database on the Ultrix machine:

    Object Volatile Summary as of Tue Jun 15 14:18:04 EDT 1993

    Object = TELL

    Number                             = 0
    File                               = /usr/bin/tell
    Type                               = Sequenced Packet
    Accept                             = Immediate 

Then copy TELL.COM over to the VMS side and define a symbol:

    $ TELL == "@some-disk:[some-dir]TELL.COM"


To support TELL from Ultrix to VMS, copy TELL.COM over to the VMS side
as above and make sure you've got object TELL defined in your NCP database
on the VMS machine:

    Object Volatile Characteristics as of 15-Jun-1993 14:21:28

    Object = TELL

    Number                   = 0
    File id                  = some-disk:[some-dir]TELL.COM


You may want to make sure that default access control doesn't let
people get access to the TELL object.  When used with DECnet proxy
access, TELL does a nice imitation of rsh.

	John Briggs				vaxs09@v7.vitro.com


