>Optimal results? What is optimal in your case amy not be optimal in mine. I use
>/Block=32256. Density is generally not relevent to the 8500 (although it should
>be). Group_Size of 10, the default, is likely adequate, but if you are
>paranoid, you can increase it. Of course, this directly inmpact the effective
>density.

Remember that with /GROUP_SIZE=n, to increase the number of redundency groups
you should *decrease* the value of n; the reduce the number of redundency 
groups you should *increase* the value of n.

The following files on-disk savesets were created using commands like:

  $ BACKUP [.TEMP]*.*;*  X.BCK_002/GROUP_SIZE=2
  $ BACKUP [.TEMP]*.*;*  X.BCK_010/GROUP_SIZE=10

and as you see, the size of the save sets goes down (hence less redundent
data being written) as the value of "n" goes up.
  
  X.BCK_002;1    4284/4284    
  X.BCK_010;1    3150/3150    
  X.BCK_050;1    2898/2898    
  X.BCK_100;1    2898/2898    

