In article <dillonwr-230693134446@17.1.7.245>, dillonwr@msgate.corp.apple.com (Bill Dillon) writes:
> I must have a modified one then because the VS3100/30 on my desk (bought in
> 1990) has both DKAxxx and DKBxxx on it.  One confusion during setup was
> that the controller on each bus (SCSI A & B) is at address 6 not SCSI 0 and
> it cannot be changed. So the available addresses are 0 - 5 & 7.  Could
> someone explain why DEC did it this way??

  Yup, the newer Model 30's all had SCSI/SCSI, while the older ones had SCSI/
ST506 if any devices were installed, otherwise they had no adapter.

  You can set the controller's address to other than 6 with the console com-
mand SET SCSIA # or SET SCSIB #. The reason that DEC used 6 as the default
was that they were studying dual-hosted configurations, where one system
would have ID 6 and the other would have ID 7.

> Also be advised that (in addition to the DEC supplied cables (that Terry
> Kennedy  talked about that end in a CENTRONICS 50 pin male connector)
> companies like ZZYXX and Andataco sell cables that end in a DB50 male
> connector that can be used with disk drives that would normally fit on a
> Sun/HP/RS6000.  I currently have a setup that uses both.  This setup also
> works well on my VS4090.

  Right, but it's unlikely that they would stock cables which have a 68-pin
hi-density female on the other end. I believe the VS4000/90 uses the BC09D 
family of cables, which has a 50-pin high-density male (normal hi-density
SCSI) on the other end, and is thus a commodity cable.

