> and allocated).  We would like to do this without spawning off a command file
> and parsing the output.  We have looked for a system call or library routine
> but can't seem to find any to fit the bill.  The file on the disk is closed and
> the priveleges should not be a factor.  Sounds like a simple problem, yet we
> can't find an "easy" way to do it.

    The "easy" way is to use SYS$OPEN and SYS$CLOSE with a properly constructed
FAB. :-)  The total space taken on disk (allocated) is at FAB$L_ALQ,
while what you call the "actual" size is the EBK (End of file BlocK) which
is gotten by chaining an XAB defined by $XABFHCDEF to the FAB.  BTW, the
documentation on these things is (I believe) in the I/O Users Reference 
Manual rather than in any of the RTL's or SS references.

    OK, it's simple if you've done it once before :-}  I've got an example
program written in Fortran which is too long to post, but which I'll be
happy to send to anyone whose interested.  It also retrieves all four
of a file's date fields.

	-Ken
