In article <1993Jun17.222317.2216@cmkrnl.com>, jeh@cmkrnl.com writes:
> I have yet to see any test program measure it this fast; 40 msec is typical. 
> But then, I've yet to see ANY drive measure as fast as its spec -- I think the
> "average" access time must be the "average of not-worst cases", or some such. 

  Back when I did disk drive work I used to know how this was computed. It's
not the obvious way, but there *is* a system to it. I seem to recall that the
brute-force method is to average all the possible seeks, while we should be
able to approximate it by averaging all the possible seek *distances* (so that
a seek from cylinder 1 to cylinder 10 should equal a seek from cylinder 2 to
cylinder 11). This normally works out pretty closely. Then the funny stuff
starts, like weighting the sample so that shorter seeks count more than longer
ones (under the theory that most seeks are short). There's a formula for this
- I'll see if I can dig it up. I believe it's related to the fact that there
are more possible short seeks than long ones (on a 1000-clyinder drive, there
is only one possible 1000-cylinder seek, while there are 999 possible 1-cyl-
inder seeks).

  On modern servoed drives, especially zone-recorded ones, it's hard to predict
the actual performance because it will vary depending on the selected head and
sector. An extreme example is the DEC RA60, where DEC claimed "no head align-
ment is needed" due to the embedded servo. Unfortunately, just dropping a head
in without coarse alignment could result in being off by several cylinders,
which required extensive servo following to correct.

