                 DECUS '93/ATLANTA NATIONAL EVENT CALLED A
                    SUCCESS BY ATTENDEES AND EXHIBITORS


        Seminars And Symposium Sessions Address Multiplatform Issues
       Response To New Trade Show Prompts Expansion Of Future Events


SHREWSBURY, Mass., June 18, 1993 --  DECUS '93/Atlanta, the spring 1993 
national seminars symposium and trade show event of the Digital Equipment 
Computer Users Society (DECUS) U.S. Chapter, was deemed a success by both 
attendees and exhibitors. The event, which ran June 5 - 11 in Atlanta, GA, 
generated over 5200 registrations.

Over 1500 of those registrations were in direct response to the new DECUS 
trade show. The 27,000 square foot trade show featured the newest leading 
edge products, services and technologies for multiplatform environments.  
The show was such a success that over 32,000 square feet of exhibit space 
has already been booked for DECUS '93/San Francisco in December, and an 
additional 27,000 square feet of space has been booked for next spring's 
DECUS national event in New Orleans.

DECUS '93/Atlanta featured a keynote address by Robert Palmer, president and 
CEO of Digital Equipment Corporation.  Over 3,700 attendees packed the 
auditorium and ballrooms of the Georgia World Congress Center to hear Palmer 
speak about the ongoing transformation Digital has undertaken to position 
itself for success in the future.

Other key Digital executives presented sessions throughout the week, 
including: Charles Christ, vice president, Storage Business Unit; Laurence 
Walker, vice president, Network Engineering Group; William Demmer, vice 
president, Computer Systems Group; and Donald Harbert, vice president, 
OpenVMS Engineering Group.

Digital also sponsored a "listening forum" which featured a panel of Digital 
vice presidents and other high level personnel. Attendees used this unique 
opportunity to voice a diverse range of comments and concerns to panel 
members.

Over 50 percent of the 1,100 technology sessions at the DECUS '93/Atlanta 
symposium addressed a number of industry-wide interoperability issues. 
Symposium sessions and seminars covered technical issues, management issues, 
applications, platforms and technologies. For the first time, DECUS offered 
a management-focused subset of the semiannual national event.

As part of its slate of multiplatform offerings, DECUS '93/Atlanta featured 
over 75 hours of in-depth technical content sessions having direct Microsoft 
relevancy.  These sessions were conducted by Microsoft and Digital 
representatives as well as by experienced product users.  

The DECUS national event also featured the Digital Technology Center which 
provided attendees with the opportunity to view and test the products, 
services and technologies of Digital and Digital-partners while meeting with 
developers, product managers and other experienced users. 

DECUS members solve their organizations' computing problems and stay 
informed abut industry issues and emerging technologies with the aid of the 
Chapter's semiannual national seminars, symposium and trade show event; 
regional conferences; local seminars program; Special Interest and Local 
Users Groups; electronic conferencing system; and public domain library. 

