Subject: Alpha 3000/400 failures due to memory board flex
Keywords: Alpha, 3000, 400, memory, failure
Message-ID: <50816@sdcc12.ucsd.edu>
Date: 11 Jun 93 05:29:29 GMT
Sender: news@sdcc12.ucsd.edu
Organization: =CSE Dept., U.C. San Diego
Lines: 32
Nntp-Posting-Host: beowulf.ucsd.edu

I have installed eight Alpha 3000/400 workstations.  Two of these suffered
immediately from random memory failures.  I believe that the problems were
caused by two design flaws in the workstation:

1. The plastic ring-tabs which stick up from the frontmost memory
   motherboard can catch on a screw which holds the CD-ROM bay in place.
   When the drive assembly is swung into place, this screw grabs the
   ring tab and pulls the memory backwards, shorting it out against the
   next memory motherboard.  (And bending it in the process)

2. The cables from the power supply to the system motherboard pass by the
   frontmost memory motherboard.  These cables are bunched up in such a
   way that when the drive plate is swung back down they jam against the
   SIMM itself and bend the SIMM and the memory motherboard towards the
   back of the unit.

I tried reassembling each workstation carefully but the memory errors came
back.  Finally I cut off the cable ties on the power cables and pushed them
down under the frontmost SIMMS so they won't bend, and I tucked the ring
tab from the front memory motherboard down where it can't catch on the
CD-ROM bay screw.  After doing this, each workstation seems to be OK.

Has anyone else had this problem?  I strongly suggest that anyone opening
a 3000/400 take a good look at the SIMMs and the cables when closing it
back up to make sure they aren't munched.

