paris@zygon.dev.cdx.mot.com (Gregory M. Paris) writes:
>Some here have suggested using a solid-state (RAM) disk instead
>of a real disk.  My mission is to find out if that's an option.
[...]
>Assume for the moment that price is no object.  We're looking
>for a RAM disk in the 200-300 megabyte range.  Suggestions?

You'd be much better off if you bought real RAM instead of putting all
that expensive ram on the end of an interface that you can't address
directly.  Do you really want to swap across a SCSI bus, even if you
don't have the seek, settling and rot-delay times?

I have seen 64meg simms for sale for the SparcStation 10.  Put 3-4 of
those in your machine and you can keep your whole data in memory.

This of course assumes you have enough control over your program and
data that you can prevent it from doing anything really stupid. 

If your problem is too much swapping then the extra ram should largely
eliminate your problems without any other work.  If your problem is too
much direct file-I/O you can keep that file in a tmpfs type
filesystem.  Then your file will be backed by system DRAM and get read
directly from that.

-wolfgang
