>> ANNOUNCEMENT <<

We would like to announce, to any interested parties, the availability of
Version 3.7 of uC++ (pronounced micro-C++), an extended C++ that provides
light-weight concurrency on uniprocessor and multiprocessor computers
running the UNIX operating system.  uC++ extends C++ in somewhat the same
way that C++ extends C.  The extensions introduce new objects that augment
the existing control flow facilities and provide for concurrency.  uC++
provides both direct and indirect communication using the traditional
routine call mechanism.  All aspects of uC++ are statically type-checked.
Furthermore, uC++ provides mechanisms for precisely controlling the order
in which requests are serviced and for postponing work on already-accepted
requests.

Version 3.7 is largely a rewrite of version 3.4.4, which was made
available on Sep 4, 1992.  The CHANGE-LOG describes the changes that have
been incorporated into this release of uC++.

>> CHANGE-LOG <<

** uC++ Version 3.6 (February 21, 1993) **

1. Part of the uC++ kernel is built using only C++ language features, while
other parts are built using uC++ language features.  Instead of trying to
be really tricky and do all this in one set of objects, the kernel was
divided into two parts, a simple part that was built using only C++, and a
complex part that was built with uC++.  Roughly speaking, the simple kernel
supports single processor execution, while the complex kernel supports
multiple processor execution, non-blocking I/O, and other higher level
kernel features.

2. Had to redo some of the shared memory stuff.  We had less success than
hoped for non-Sun architectures.  On the Sequent, we backed off and went
back to using their shared memory scheme.  On the SGI, we just gave up for
now.  On the Sun, we have finished the implementation, but are working
through some very difficult bugs.

3. Started writing some man pages with Murray's help.

4. Improved the installation mechanisms.  Now, all you have to do is make
changes to the top level Makefile, and you can order exactly what things
will be built and where they will be stored.

5. Ported u++-3.6 to g++-2.3.  People were having difficulty compiling the
I/O libraries of u++-3.4.4 with g++-2.3.  GNU keep changing their
interfaces.  We think all should be okay for a while now.

6. We now support the -inline flag.

** uC++ Version 3.5.1 (December 10, 1992) **

1. Added support for multiple processor version on any machine that can map
memory to be shared and private.  Instead of relying on the special
primitives shared memory directives on a host operating system, we use maps
to implement both shared and private memory.

** uC++ Version 3.5 (September 9, 1992) **

1. Moved the source code around a bit for the purposes of making the
installation of the package more versatile.  The 'src' directory now
contains the source for the kernel, library, documentation, and manual
pages.  When the package is built, the directories 'bin', 'lib', 'inc',
'doc', and 'man' will be created containing all executables, libraries,
header files, documentation and man pages.  This has a couple of benefits.
First, the 'src' directory could be completely removed after a successful
build.  Second, the software can be built over the network by creating a
'u++' directory with a link via the network to the 'src' directory.  The
source code during the build would be obtained from the network, but after
the build was complete, all the pieces would be local.

2. Added a prototype for exception handling.  Not yet documented or ready
to be used.

>> REQUIREMENTS <<

uC++ requires at least version 3.8 of dmake, which is available by
anonymous ftp from the following location (remember to set your ftp mode to
binary):

	plg.uwaterloo.ca:pub/dmake/dmake38.tar.Z

The installation instructions for uC++ also describe how to build dmake.

uC++ requires at least GNU C++ 2.3.3.  uC++ will NOT compile using other
compilers. Currently, the AT&T compiler has several deficiencies that make
it too difficult to generate reasonable code for it.  GNU C++ can be
obtained by anonymous ftp from many ftp sites.

>> FTP SITE <<

Version 3.7 of uC++ can be obtained by anonymous ftp from the following
location (remember to set your ftp mode to binary):

	plg.uwaterloo.ca:pub/uSystem/u++-3.7.tar.Z

The README file contains all the installation instructions. Good luck and
happy concurrency.

>> REFERENCE MANUAL <<

A postscript version of uC++ manual is available in the same ftp directory
as uC++, called u++-3.7.ps.Z.  It contains a copy of the uC++
documentation that has been formatted for 8.5x11 paper using LaTeX and then
converted to postscript.  This has been made available because some people
have had problems LaTeXing the documentation that comes with uC++
distribution.  Normally, it is unnecessary to retrieve this file as uC++
comes with the reference manual.

>> PORTABILITY <<

uC++ comes configured to run on any of the following platforms without
modification:

	sequent-i386 (both single and multiple processor)
	sun-sparc (both single and multiple processor, SunOS 4.1.2)
	sun-m68k
	dec-vax
	dec-mips
	mips-mips
	sgi-mips (both single and multiple processor)
	ibm-rs6000
	hp-hppa

If you wish to port uC++ to another platform, it has been know to work on
UNIX BSD 4.3, UNIX System V that has BSD system calls "setitimer" and a
"sigcontext" passed to signal handlers which contains the location of the
interrupted program, versions of QNIX and LINIX for PCs, SUN OS, ULTRIX,
DYNIX and IRIX.

>> PAPERS <<

There is a paper about uC++ version 3.2.5 in: Software--Practice and
Experience, 22(2):137-172, February 1992.

>> EXPERIENCE <<

uC++ is being used to build a database and visualization toolkit here at
Waterloo.  It is also being used for teaching purposes at Waterloo and at
other Universities.

>> FUTURE <<

The next release of uC++ will come with a visualization and monitoring
toolkit that works with X/Motif. As well, the database toolkit should be
ready for initial testing.

>> uSystem <<

If you are still using the uSystem, our C light-weight thread package, it is
still available at the same ftp location as uC++. The old ftp site, watmsg, no
longer exists.


