NNTP-Posting-Host: top.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu
Keywords: memory swap

The virtual process sizes on DEC OSF/1 reflect the sizes of the shared
libraries that are linked into the executables.  The virtual sizes don't
have any direct bearing on the actual memory or swap consumption, though,
since the bulk of the pages in use are shared.  Static initialized data in
shared libraries DOES count against the RSS (a much more accurate view of
the actual memory consumption of a given process), but only IF the machine is
in "eager" swap mode.

Eager swap is the classical BSD behaviour.  It essentially requires vm objects
to be backed up with page space on allocation, whether or not the object will
ever be used.  This is inefficient in some regards.  Consider, for example, a
case in which you put 1GB of ram into a machine in order to insure that you
NEVER page.  With eager swap, you have to allocate a huge (but almost
certainly unused) swap space.

DEC OSF/1 also allows the use of "lazy" swap evaluation.  This is done by 
removing /sbin/swapdefault (a symlink to the primary swap device) and
rebooting.  In this mode, page space is allocated on actual demand as opposed
to when the virtual object is created.  This means that for situations like
the above, the virtual memory becomes effectively the size of ram plus the
page space - a much more effective utilization of resources.  The down side to
lazy evaluation is that you can over-commit page space.  In that scenario
(like the machine I'm on - a 3000/500 with 256MB of memory, but 127 MB of
swap) I can allocate memory until I run out, then things start to page. 
The thing is, I can have more things allocated than I have page space
for.  If that happens, some processes will likely die an unpleasant death. 

Both methods have their points, both good and bad.  Eager swap generates more
deterministic behavior, but is somewhat less resource efficient.  I'd tend to
recommend it for, say, a database server in a commercial application.  Lazy
swap is great for code development, or for stuff doing sparse arrays, and
other applications where large VM objects get allocated but only smally pieces
actually get touched.  The nice thing is that DEC OSF/1 does both - I think it
is the only UNIX implementation from a major vendor that does that ;-)

Your mileage will vary, and the method that is best depends on your
application.  It is very easy to change, though, by deleting or restoring 
the symlink and rebooting.

Eric Osborne
