In article <CHRIS.93Jun9153049@Pegasus.aquarius.cc.ucf.edu>, chris@aquarius.cc.ucf.edu (Chris Maukonen) writes:
> From what I understand there is one "driver" being used and it is
> a new one. If they start the decnet and JNET apps the the sequence
> recomended in the docs on JNET, JNET will not even start up. Giving
> the message that "The device is not available or in use". If they
> start it up the oppisite way (JNET first then decnet), JNET 
> comes up with the line showing "active". However when a protocol
> analizer is put on the serial line out, it shows the vax not
> sending any data at all.
> 
> What I would like to know is if anyone has gone through this type
> of conversion before and what might have been forgotin. 

  It would have been helpful if you had posted which interface card you
were using as well as the versions of the software involved. Let me give
you some brief pointers and then you can try again with more info if you
need to.

  There are 3 possible sync protocols in Jnet - VMB, NJE, and something
else (I forget - but it isn't common). Most of the old cards do both
VMB and NJE, while many of the newer cards will only do NJE. If you have
a DSV11 or similar interface, you'll need the Wide Area Network Device
Drivers (WANDD) kit. You have to be careful as there are many flavors of
this kit - some for DECnet Phase V, some for PSI, etc. Calling Wingra
(the Jnet folks) should get you the right info on which kit is needed.

  You can't just take your existing JANLINKS.JCP (and possibly JANLOAD.COM)
files and use them - you'll have to re-configure them. If you don't have a
lot of effort invested in local customizations, I'd just run JANCONFIG.COM
and use what it gives you - it's pretty good.

  If you need to change your external link from VMB to NJE, you'll need to
tell your neighbor about it as they'll need to change their definitions as
well.

