Richard,

In article <C8BCtw.563@sheridanc.on.ca>, richard@eclipse.sheridanc.on.ca (Richard Baird) writes:
> 
> I'm curious about the internal structure of the DEC 3000 series. My DEC 
> marketdroid has informed me that all traffic from the CPU (including
> memory references) uses the TURBOchannel bus. Seems to me I read 
> somewhere that these machines actually had a samll version of a 
> crossbar switch to route the traffic between memory-cpu-Turbochannel,
> and that the Turbochannel was simply an I/O expansion bus.
> Is this correct or has DEC used the Turbochannel for everything?
> 
> Along the same lines, does traffic to the imbedded SCSI and Ethernet
> use the Turbochannel, or is Turbochannel just used for expansion?
> 
> Is there a DEC document which describes the bus layout on these machines
> that I can ftp from somewhere (a block diagram showing the functional units
> and the bandwidth between them would be great)?
> 
> 
> I find it hard to believe that DEC would build a machine with 
> a 100 MB/s system bus, when SUN, HP, etc are using 300+ MB/s system
> busses in comparably priced systems, especially when the 21064 is
> screaming along at 100-150 MHz.
> 
Richard,

You should look on gatekeeper.dec.com in the /pub/DEC/DECinfo/technical-summary directory:

	dec-3000-400.ps & .ps.Z		DEC 3000 model 400 AXP Workstation
	dec-3000-500.ps & .ps.Z		DEC 3000 Model 500 AXP Workstation

These two technical summaries are from the Nov 1992 timeframe and have lots
of system diagrams.  In short, memory access is not done with the TURBOchannel,
but just about everything else is -- SCSI, Audio, Graphics, etc.   

Unfortunately, we do not have similar documents for the Models 300 and 300L 
yet, but the idea is the same with one exception -- the Models 300/300L have imbedded graphics on the motherboard and do not use the TURBOchannel.  The 
Model 500X is a just a model 500 with one less TURBOchannel slot and the faster 200 MHz Alpha chip. 

-- 
Russ Jones
UNIX Marketroid
Digital Equipment Corporation


