In article <1v7falINNbv4@ope001.iao.ford.com> mjo@iao.ford.com (Mike O'Connor) writes:

   I know that:

   How would I go about actually *installing* the Sun RPCs on an Ultrix
   system?  Besides general stability, my concerns would be:

   1.  I don't wish to clobber any of the Apollo/DCE RPC supported stuff.
       If at all possible, I'd like the Sun stuff to run concurrently.

   2.  I don't wish to break any of the supported applications, like
       NIS, that use the Sun RPC code.

   I'd like to get some Sun RPC functionality up and running, ultimately,
   to integrate these machines into an environment that mostly uses Sun
   RPCs.  Any pointers in the right direction, preferably from people
   who've actually attempted this, would be most appreciated.  Thanks!

I have done this before without problems (granted it was a couple of
years and software revisions ago.)  The ONC RPC is orthogonal to the
DCE RPC.  For both systems, RPC stuff consists of libraries, a couple
of development idl, rpcgen, etc. and a few daemons (portmapper,
location broker).  These items do not overlap between ONC and DCE.
You might have collision with some of the supplied parts of ONC.  At
least in old Ultrix, the ONC RPC library calls are in libc.a.  You
would have to link your stuff before libc.a to get the correct
version of the rpc runtime objects.  You would have to place your
version of rpcgen in your path before the system supplied version.  My
suggestion is that you don't bother to have a custom version of the
portmapper, but rather go with the one supplied by the system.

regards,
fletcher





