>We've got an application running here as part of a configuration
>mamagement system and it's suffering unacceptable performance.  The
>problem is with regard to a data file of currently about 150 megabytes
>that this application reads from and writes to very intensively.

	Have you carefully examined whatever algorithms you are using
for storage and access? If it's an application you control, improving
cache management in the application itself will almost always give
better results than adding memory to the system cache. Analysis of
the access patterns of the data might give interesting results
too. If it's read or write heavy, have you considered just using
faster disks? Something like the Turbostor IPI controllers from
Genroco will give a significant speedup over cheap SCSI disks
on a DECsystem's embedded controller. Depending on the access
patterns in your application, IPI might be a big win, because of
the kind of optimizations it performs (which help a lot in
some types of operations). A non-parity checked striped set with
SCSI disks with large on-disk caches might give the speedup
you need, too.

	I know this sounds obnoxious, but I've often seen people
throw hardware at situations when it's their application that is
the problem. Of course, if you can't change your application,
then you *are* stuck. There's a company called DES (Drive Emulation
Systems, or something like that) that sells a 50usec access time
SCSI-2 RAM disk that can be up to 200Mb or so. They advertise
sometimes in Sun World, I think...

mjr.


