scocpb@thor.cf.ac.uk (Chris Brown) writes:
>I'm in the process of installing AF2R2. I plan to install it on my workstation for
>appraisal and to play around with multi-media mail. My problem is that I'm working
>under DECathena. All of the kernal configuration files are mounted up from a server, as
>are the device files.

Device files are *not* obtained from the syslib server under DECathena - they
live on the local root filesystem under /dev, created during the client
install.  Ditto for /vmunix itself.  Once client install is completed,
root and /var are entirely local, and if it's your own personal workstation
you should be free to change it as you wish.

As for the kernel config files, there's no real magic needed... All you
should need to do is to create a local version of /sys (in fact, recent
versions of DECathena do exactly this in order to create a kernel on-the-fly
during client install if need be).  Here's a short how-to:
- Remove the /sys symbolic link that points to /usr/sys.
- Create a local /sys directory.  Actually, you may be better off creating
a /var/sys, and setting up a symbolic link /sys -> var/sys, since there's
likely to be much more room in /var than root.
- From this point on, you need to either copy stuff from /usr/sys to /var/sys,
or create symbolic links underneath /var/sys that point to /usr/sys, in order
to create a complete /sys tree on your local disk.  The /sys/conf tree must
be copied in its entirety (though you can throw away any locally created
kernel config files you don't need).  /sys/MIPS must be a local directory.
From underneath /sys/MIPS, you'll need the Makefile and the machine symlink.
The /sys/MIPS/BINARY directory is needed in one form or another - if you're
going to be adding or replacing files there, the directory itself will have
to be local, but you can symlink all the *.o files to save space; if you're
not going to be messing with the contents of that directory, make the
directory itself a symlink.  All the other directories under /sys can probably
be symlinked, unless you need to make changes to one or more of them, in which
case copy them.
- After that, you should be able to create config files and run doconfig or
config to your heart's content, same as any other ULTRIX box.

Hope this helps...

-Shawn Mamros
E-mail to: mamros@athena.lkg.dec.com


