In article <1ugsep$ep2@news1.digex.net>, mcguire@rocinante.digex.net (Dave McGuire) writes:
|>Tony Konashenok (avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu) wrote:
|>: Does VS2000 format its disks in RQDX1/2 type format,
|>: RQDX3 type format, or its own one?
|>
|>  The VS2000 formats its disks in rqdx3 format.  I have sucessfully
|>loaded a system disk for a VS2000 on a MicroVAX II with an rqdx3.

Note that while the RQDX3 uses 1:1 interleaving, the VS2000 uses 2:1 
interleaving.  What this means is that a disk formatted on the RQDX3
and placed in a VS2000 will run very slow as the sectors will go by
to fast for the VS2000 to read.  Conversely, a disk from a VS2000 on
a RQDX3 will only be somewhat slower since the access time per sector
doubles.
