In article <1uvri1INNsvs@flash.pax.tpa.com.au>, msmith@flash.pax.tpa.com.au (miff) writes:

|>Hithere!
|>	I've seen people discussing the KA650 CPU board here, but I've
|>missed anyone actually explaining what it is...
|>
|>Is it a Qbus device? Is it any faster than a KA630? Would one work in a
|>MVII? (BA123 enclosure/matching backplane, lots of power left over) Any other
|>pros or cons?
|>
|>Thanks!
|>mike
|>-- 

The KA650 CPU module was successor to the MicroVAX II.  It used the first
CMOS implementation of the VAX architecture, and was around 3 times as fast
as the MicroVAX II (KA630).  Like the KA630, the KA650 plugs into the 
first slot of a Qbus backplane and assumes the role of arbiter on the Qbus.
Also like the KA630, the KA650 had memory modules that plugged into adjacent
quad-height Qbus slots.  The KA630 could take a max of 2 memory modules, the 
KA650 could take 4.  The C-D interconnect in the backplane was used to carry
RAS/CAS signals to the memory module, and a separate ribbon cable carried the
data and parity(MS630) or ECC(MS650).  

Although the MS630 and MS650 memory modules are similar in concept, they are 
not interchangeable.

Regards,

>>>Joe


