(miff) writes:
> 	I've seen people discussing the KA650 CPU board here, but I've
> missed anyone actually explaining what it is...

The KA650 is the CPU card from a MicroVAX 3500 or 3600.  (Or, if a workstation
video display is installed, it may call itself a VAXstation 3200 or 3600.)  It
will work in place of a KA630 CPU card (even the connector for the console
distribution panel is compatible), and will indeed drop into a BA23 or BA123
backplane (uses the same Q/CD slots as the KA630)... but as someone else
correctly stated in the "KA650 with MS630 memory?" thread, you will also have
to replace the MicroVAX II's memory modules with MS650-xx units. 

The KA650 is a little less than three times faster than the KA630.  Put it
this way:  The KA630 is next to unusable under VMS 5.5 and DECwindows/Motif,
while the KA650 is quite usable, even with an incoming uucp feed. 

Terry Kennedy reports that a KA650 that isn't up to rev. K is next to
worthless; before this rev, there were problems with some Q-bus devices and
interesting limitations on writing to Q-bus mapping registers. Also rev K gave
the ROMs the ability to boot from a DSSI disk (using a Q-bus DSSI adapter).  I
know lots of sites that ran for years on un-revved KA650's, so I wouldn't say
"next to worthless", just "be aware of what the unit will and won't do".  It
is probably true that due to concerns over this, the *resale* value of a 
non-rev-K KA650 is pretty low, so don't pay much for one.  

