In article <1uvri1INNsvs@flash.pax.tpa.com.au>, msmith@flash.pax.tpa.com.au (miff) writes:
> 	I've seen people discussing the KA650 CPU board here, but I've
> missed anyone actually explaining what it is...
> 
> Is it a Qbus device? Is it any faster than a KA630? Would one work in a
> MVII? (BA123 enclosure/matching backplane, lots of power left over) Any other
> pros or cons?

  It's the "MicroVAX III" board, found in the MicroVAX 3500 and 3600, as well
as the VAXstation 3200. It's a nice board, 2.8 VUPS (compared to 0.9 for the
MicroVAX II).

  It is a normal Q-bus board and uses the same cabinet kit (rear panel, if
you will) as the MicroVAX II. The memory is different, and if you have the
FCO'd MicroVAX II memory cable (shielded) that won't work right in the III
either. However, MicroVAX III memory is dirt cheap, as it is the same *style*
as is used on newer systems, but too slow, so it is selling for under the
cost of the RAM chips - I've been paying about $50 for genuine-DEC 8Mb ECC
boards.

  There are a _lot_ of these floating around, many of them surplus from a
big aerospace outfits closed-down CAD operation. They come in two flavors -
timeshare (multi-user) and server (single-user, sort of - read the DEC Terms
and Conditions for the exact gory details). If the board is in a box with
graphics hardware, then it's the VAXstation variant, using the server CPU.

  The thing to watch out for on these is the hardware revision - if it isn't
Rev. K or higher it will likely not work with most 3rd-party peripherals.
The repair/upgrade cost on one of these (from DEC) is more than the board is
worth on the market today. The fix requires changing one of the gate arrays
on the board, so it is *very* unlikely that you can get this fix from any of
the 3rd-party repair outfits.

