  I don't know what the export situation is like these days, but here in the
US there are a number of dealers known for having low prices on stuff like
this. You might try Computer Clearing House in New York (I don't have their
number handy, but they advertise in many of the DEC-specific magazines).

> How do you find out which rev. it is? I've got a KA650 (another one)
> which I couldn't use together with an Emulex UC08 controller. Maybe
> it worked, but I couldn't get into the FRD/NovRam Setup.
> On the cable connecting the memory to the CPU, there was a label
> saying Rev. A01. I hope that isn't the Rev. of the CPU.
> 
> Can I find out which revision I've got from looking at the firmware
> version showed at boot time?

  The revision should be stamped into the back (solder side) of the board's
handle. In general, this will be something like nnnxm where nnn is a 3-digit
date code (not present on most newer modules), x is the revision letter, and
m is a sub-revision number, usually not important. [Usually, it indicates
how the board got to the current revision. Sometimes it indicates a firmware
revision, like on HSC channel cards].

  The latest firmware supports SET HOST/DUP, but I don't know if that's a
sure way to tell - the new ROMs may work on older boards.

