>
>The dump command has started to misbehave. Up until a little while ago,
>dump was working as well as dump ever does; however, any invocation of
>dump (on server or client) now produces the output:
>
>	DUMP: SIGSEGV()  ABORTING!
>	Illegal instruction (core dumped)
>
>This is produced even if no dump is done (-S option), or for any output
>device. No hardware or software changes were made in between the times
>when dump was working and when the above started happening. 
>
>I have replaced /bin/dump with a known good version from a different
>system, to no effect. Presumably something has got corrupted somewhere,
>although I cannot find any evidence of this.
>
>Anyone know where I should look next? Thanks.
>

Many thanks to all those who replied, especially Alan Rollow (alan@
nabeth.cxo.dec.com) who was first with the right answer. My problem
was that I had imported filesystems from a VMS machine whose remote
name did not begin with a slash; dump does not like this, and appears
not to contain the requisite error checking. For example:

	vger8@vger:/fsys/vger/disk8:rw:0:0:nfs:bg,nosuid:

Upon adding the leading slash (which fortunately does not confuse
the VMS system), everything was fine. One other possibility that
was mentioned was that /etc/dumpdates was missing or incorrectly
formatted; mine was OK.

